\title{Bivariate evaluation vectors and the two triangles}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{definition}
\meta{defines}{bivariate polynomial space; evaluation vector; two-triangle pattern}
\meta{dependencies}{[Interpolation nodes](cn-0063)}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Lemma 4.1, equation (4.1); reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026. [Lean source: TwoTriangleIndices](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/TwoTriangleIndices.lean).}

\p{Fix #{s\geq1}, #{n=2s+1}, and distinct real [interpolation nodes](cn-0063) #{x_1,\ldots,x_n}. The \em{bivariate polynomial space} used here consists of #{P(u,v)} of degree at most #{s-1} in each variable separately. Its dimension is #{s^2}, with basis #{u^a v^b}, #{0\leq a,b<s}. Its \em{evaluation vector} at #{(x_p,x_q)} is #{h_{pq}=(x_p^a x_q^b)_{0\leq a,b<s}}.}

\p{The \em{two-triangle pattern} is}

##{Q_s=\{(p,q):1\leq q<p\leq s+1\}\ \cup\ \{(p,q):s+1<p<q\leq2s+1\}.}

\p{It has #{s(s+1)/2+s(s-1)/2=s^2} positions. For #{s=2}, it is #{\{(2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(4,5)\}}. The order of the nodes need not agree with their numerical sizes. This freedom is crucial when [relabeling spoke orders](cn-0034).}

\p{For #{s=3}, the selected positions are the bullets in this grid (row #{p}, column #{q}). The proof first removes row #{4}, then column #{1}; exchanging the remaining groups and swapping coordinates leaves the pattern for #{s=2}.}

##{\begin{array}{c|ccccccc}
 &1&2&3&4&5&6&7\\\hline
1&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot\\
2&\bullet&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot\\
3&\bullet&\bullet&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot\\
4&\bullet&\bullet&\bullet&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot\\
5&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\bullet&\bullet\\
6&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\bullet\\
7&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot&\cdot
\end{array}}
